{
  "teamId": 134,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Pittsburgh Pirates opened their season with a gut punch. On Opening Day, Luis Robert Jr. launched a three-run homer off Hunter Barco, and the early weeks were defined by a bullpen that couldn't hold leads and an offense prone to killing its own rallies. The Pirates stumbled through April, watching opponents capitalize on late-inning mistakes while Pittsburgh's hitters grounded into double plays at the worst possible moments. It was a rocky start that left the club searching for an identity.\n\nMay and June brought signs of life — and signs of trouble existing side by side. Bryan Reynolds delivered an enormous two-run walk-off homer on May 29th that reminded everyone of his star power, and Henry Davis's grand slam in early June hinted at a potent lineup lurking beneath the surface. But the bullpen remained a persistent wound. Gregory Soto surrendered three-run homers, Evan Sisk gave up critical doubles, and Mason Montgomery was torched for a pivotal two-run double that swung a late June game. The Pirates felt like a team that could beat anyone on a given night but couldn't string wins together consistently.\n\nThe second half offered genuine hope. Esmerlyn Valdez's grand slam in July and a crucial double play turned by Gregory Soto that preserved a lead showed Pittsburgh capable of winning ugly when it mattered. Brandon Lowe emerged as one of the team's most reliable offensive forces, smashing home runs through August and September — his 27th and 33rd round-trippers arriving at clutch moments. Rafael Flores Jr. added punch from an unexpected source, and Oneil Cruz's moonshot in late August gave the Pirates life in a tight pennant chase.\n\nIn the end, the Pirates finished 82-80 — a winning record that felt bittersweet. A two-run homer by Eduardo Valencia off Mason Montgomery on the penultimate weekend of the season served as a brutal reminder of how thin the margins were all year. Pittsburgh scored more runs than they allowed, hit 186 home runs, and played competitive baseball from wire to wire, yet it wasn't enough to crack the playoff field. The 2026 Pirates were a team that proved they belonged — now they have to prove they can take the next step.",
